.TH BOSWAS-CLOCK 1 "November 2026" "boswas-apps 3.0~alpha1" "Boswas OS"
.SH NAME
boswas-clock \- alarms, timers, stopwatch, world clock and focus sessions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B boswas-clock
.RB [ \-\-page
.IR page ]
.br
.B boswas-clock
.BR \-\-version " | " \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B boswas-clock
is Clock, the clock application of the Boswas desktop. It has five pages.
.TP
.B Focus sessions
A session of 5 to 240 minutes, split into focus periods and short breaks
(or run without breaks). A notification tells when each period ends. The
page shows the focus time of today against a daily goal, the time of
yesterday and the number of days in a row on which the goal was reached.
.TP
.B Timers
Any number of named timers that run at the same time, each with a ring
that shows the time left and the time of day it ends. A timer can be
paused, reset, edited, deleted and enlarged to fill the page.
.TP
.B Alarms
Alarms with a time, a name, the weekdays on which they repeat (none: the
alarm rings once) and a snooze time. Each alarm shows how long it is until
it rings and can be switched off without deleting it.
.TP
.B Stopwatch
Start, pause and reset; laps with their own time and the total; the
fastest and the slowest lap are marked.
.TP
.B World clock
The local time and the time in places chosen from a list of cities and of
the zones of the system's time\-zone database, with day or night and the
difference to the local time. A slider shows the times in all places at
another time, up to a day before or after now.
.PP
Alarms, timers and focus sessions are announced by
.BR boswas\-reminders (1),
a small service of the desktop session, so they are announced while Clock
is closed. Clock starts the service when it is not running. Timers, the
stopwatch and focus sessions go on while Clock is closed or the computer
sleeps: they are measured on the clock that counts the time since boot
(suspended time included) and, across a restart of the computer, on the
wall clock.
.PP
Only one Clock runs per session; starting it again brings the window
forward and shows the page asked for.
It has one window, as Clock has on Windows: the alarms, timers, stopwatch
and focus session run in it, and a second window would only show them
twice. A start with
.B \-\-new\-window
therefore brings the window forward as well, and the desktop entry offers no
\(lqNew Window\(rq.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-page " page"
Open with a page:
.BR focus ,
.BR timers ,
.BR alarms ,
.BR stopwatch ,
.B world
or
.BR settings .
Without the option Clock opens the page that was open last.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print a summary of the options and exit.
.SH KEYS
Ctrl+N adds an alarm, a timer or a city on the page that is open. On the
stopwatch, Space starts and pauses, L records a lap and R resets. Ctrl+Z
takes back a deletion. Ctrl+Tab and Ctrl+Shift+Tab go through the pages,
Ctrl+, opens the settings, F11 switches full screen, Ctrl+W and Ctrl+Q
close Clock.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/com.boswas.Clock/clock.json
Alarms, timers, the stopwatch, the focus session with its history, and
the places of the world clock. Clock and the reminder service change the
file under a lock
.RI ( clock.json.lock )
and replace it atomically. A file that cannot be read is set aside as
.IR clock.json.damaged .
.TP
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/com.boswas.Clock/settings.json
The settings: time format, sound, snooze time, focus period and break.
.TP
.I $XDG_STATE_HOME/com.boswas.Clock/window.ini
The size of the window and the page that was open.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (also when a running Clock was brought forward); 4 when started
as root, which Clock refuses.
.SH NOTES
The sound of alarms is a chime that the reminder service generates; there
is no choice of sounds. Do not disturb is a setting of the desktop and is
not changed by focus sessions.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR boswas\-reminders (1),
.BR boswas\-calendar (1)
